{"word": "reindeer", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "reindeer", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɹeɪndɪə/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɹeɪndɪɹ/", "tags": ["General American"]}], "forms": [{"word": "reindeer", "tags": ["plural"]}, {"word": "reindeers", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(plural: reindeer) Any Arctic and subarctic-dwelling deer of the species Rangifer tarandus, with a number of subspecies.", "tags": ["plural"], "examples": ["Santa Claus' sleigh is supposedly pulled by nine reindeer"], "quotes": [{"text": "Here is a prodigious number of wild beaſts, as ſtags, bears, wolves, foxes of various colours, martens, hares, glittens, beavers, otters, elk, and rein deer: the latter is leſs than a stag.", "reference": "1768, D[aniel] Fenning, “LAPLAND”, in The Royal English Dictionary; or, A Treasury of the English Language, 3rd improved edition, London: Printed for R. Baldwin, Hawes and Co., T. Caslon, S. Crowder, J. Johnson, Wilson and Fell, Robinson and Roberts, and B. Collins, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "The rein-deer, which in Scandinavia can scarcely exist to the south of the sixty-fifth parallel, descends, in consequence of the greater coldness of the climate, to the fiftieth degree, in Chinese Tartary, and often roves into a country of more southern latitude than any part of England.", "reference": "1832, Charles Lyell, chapter VI, in Principles of Geology: Being an Attempt to Explain the Former Changes of the Earth's Surface, by Reference to Causes Now in Operation, volume II, London: John Murray, →OCLC, page 94:"}, {"text": "Herds of wandering reindeer are frequently seen, and may even hold up the train while they cross the unfenced line to reach their feeding grounds.", "reference": "1958 December 25, M. D. Greville and H. A. Vallance, “Sweden's Inland Railway”, in Railway Magazine, page 829:"}, {"